{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tree shaking tree shaking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md","fileName":"Tree Shaking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tree Shaking","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 20:37\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Front-End Service Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Front-End Service Design","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","aspnetcore3andangular9_3ed.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tree shaking tree shaking","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tree Shaking.md","fileName":"Tree Shaking.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tree Shaking","heading":"Tree Shaking","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Tree shaking is a build optimization that removes unused code from the final JavaScript bundle. Smaller bundles reduce code transferred to and processed by the browser.\n\nAngular services provided through root-level injectable metadata can participate in this optimization because the build system can determine whether the service is used. Registering code in ways that create unavoidable references can prevent removal.\n\nThe .NET Core 3 desktop discussion applies the same removal idea to application publication: unused runtime components can be excluded while producing a compact deployment, including a single executable. The result depends on the publisher being able to determine which code is reachable.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Front-End Service Design","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","aspnetcore3andangular9_3ed.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes